nWrap"><strong>Object
            Classification Wrapper</strong></a> groups objects together
            on the basis of shared characteristics for discovery. It
            contains the information groups for <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectWorkTypeWrap">Object/Work
            Type</a> and <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#classificationWrap">Classification</a>
            with the index elements &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectWorkType">lido:objectWorkType</a>&gt;
            and &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#classification">lido:classification</a>&gt;.
            While the former describes the object or work type as
            specific as possible, the latter groups objects together by
            broad categories, particularly for browsing or facet
            filtering. For information on the index elements &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectWorkType">lido:objectWorkType</a>&gt;
            and &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#classification">lido:classification</a>&gt;
            see sections <a href="#mandatory-elements"><em>5.1 Mandatory
            elements</em></a> and <a
            href="#recommended-elements"><em>5.2 Recommended
            elements</em></a>.</p>
            <h4 data-number="4.4.1.2" id="object-identification"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.4.1.2</span> Object
            Identification</h4>
            <p>The <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectIdentificationWrap"><strong>Object
            Identification Wrapper</strong></a> contains identifying
            information about an item, including its title, repository
            information, a textual object description, and description
            of the physical appearance. A minimum of information in this
            area is necessary to identify the unique item described in
            the LIDO record, distinguishing it from all similar ones in
            a collection, a local database, or an aggregation portal.
            For information on &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#titleSet">lido:titleSet</a>&gt;,
            &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectMeasurements">lido:objectMeasurements</a>&gt;,
            and &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#materialsTech">lido:materialsTech</a>&gt;
            see sections <a href="#mandatory-elements"><em>5.1 Mandatory
            elements</em></a> and <a
            href="#recommended-elements"><em>5.2 Recommended
            elements</em></a>.</p>
            <h4 data-number="4.4.1.3" id="event"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.4.1.3</span> Event</h4>
            <p>The <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#eventWrap"><strong>Event
            Wrapper</strong></a> contains the repeatable &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#eventSet">lido:eventSet</a>&gt;
            and enables the description of various events the object or
            work has been involved in or is associated with in some way.
            The &lt;lido:eventSet&gt; itself may be equally repeated in
            order to record the history of an object and its provenance
            as a chain of custodial events. An event-centric cataloging
            opens up ways to establish a network of contextual entities
            that may be associated with the event, usually actors,
            places, and dates. The LIDO elements &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#eventActor">lido:eventActor</a>&gt;,
            &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#eventPlace">lido:eventPlace</a>&gt;,
            and &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#eventDate">lido:eventDate</a>&gt;
            reflect the high-level entities of the CIDOC CRM model, <a
            href="https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.1_with_translations.html#E39">E39
            Actor</a>, <a
            href="https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.1_with_translations.html#E53">E53
            Place</a>, and <a
            href="https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.1_with_translations.html#E52">E52
            Time-Span</a>, respectively. For information on indexing
            events see section <a href="#recommended-elements"><em>5.2
            Recommended elements</em></a>.</p>
            <h4 data-number="4.4.1.4" id="object-relation"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.4.1.4</span> Object
            Relation</h4>
            <p>The <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectRelationWrap"><strong>Object
            Relation Wrapper</strong></a> comprises information on the
            topics of the object in focus, i.e., what it is about, and
            possible relations to other objects or works. It contains
            the two information groups &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#subjectWrap">lido:subjectWrap</a>&gt;
            and &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#relatedWorksWrap">lido:relatedWorksWrap</a>&gt;.
            For information on the index elements &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#subject">lido:subject</a>&gt;
            and &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#relatedWork">lido:relatedWork</a>&gt;
            see sections <a href="#mandatory-elements"><em>5.1 Mandatory
            elements</em></a> and <a
            href="#recommended-elements"><em>5.2 Recommended
            elements</em></a>.</p>
            <h3 data-number="4.4.2" id="administrative-metadata-1"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.4.2</span> Administrative
            Metadata</h3>
            <p><a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#administrativeMetadata"><strong>Administrative
            Metadata</strong></a> in LIDO relate to the management of
            the described record or resource, and associated usage or
            intellectual property rights. This includes information
            about the rights holder, preservation and archiving
            processes the record or resource has gone through, as well
            as technical characterstics, e.g., for decoding or rendering
            the digital representation.</p>
            <p>Information in the Administrative Metadata part of the
            LIDO schema is divided into three information areas, each of
            which has its dedicated wrapper element.</p>
            <ol type="1">
            <li><strong>Rights for Work</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Record</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Resource</strong></li>
            </ol>
            <h4 data-number="4.4.2.1" id="rights-for-work"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.4.2.1</span> Rights for
            Work</h4>
            <p>The <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#rightsWorkWrap"><strong>Rights
            for Work Wrapper</strong></a> contains information about
            intellectual property and usage rights associated with the
            described object or work. “Work” here refers to the object
            at hand as either a distinct intellectual or artistic
            creation such as a human-made artifact, or as a natural
            object such as a plant or a mineral specimen. Being an
            object in its own right, it is distinct from any surrogate
            resource which is to be described in its own wrapper. The
            rights associated with the described object or work are